'''Andromeda X''' (And 10) is a [[dwarf spheroidal galaxy]] about [[1 E22 m|2.9 million]] [[light-year]]s away from the [[Sun]] in the [[constellation]] [[Andromeda (constellation)|Andromeda]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Andromeda X |url=http://spider.seds.org/spider/LG/and10.html |access-date=2022-04-06 |website=spider.seds.org}}</ref>  Discovered in 2005 by Zucker et al., And X is a [[satellite galaxy]] of the [[Andromeda Galaxy]] (M31). Aided by the application of [[Photometry (astronomy)|stellar photometry]] to data from the [[Sloan Digital Sky Survey]] similar to the [[Andromeda IX]] discovery, the new finding indicates that this type of extremely faint satellite might be common in the [[Local Group]], potentially providing further support for hierarchical [[cold dark matter]] models.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Zucker |first1=Daniel B. |last2=Kniazev |first2=Alexei Y. |last3=Martinez-Delgado |first3=David |last4=Bell |first4=Eric F. |last5=Rix |first5=Hans-Walter |last6=Grebel |first6=Eva K. |last7=Holtzman |first7=Jon A. |last8=Walterbos |first8=Rene A. M. |last9=Rockosi |first9=Constance M. |last10=York |first10=Donald G. |last11=Barentine |first11=J. C. |date=2006-01-26 |title=Andromeda X, A New Dwarf Spheroidal Satellite of M31: Photometry |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |volume=659 |pages=L21–L24 |language=en |doi=10.1086/516748|arxiv=astro-ph/0601599 |s2cid=1064929 }}</ref>

