'''Pisces VII''' ('''Psc VII'''), or '''Triangulum III''' ('''Tri III'''), is an [[Dwarf galaxy#Ultra-faint dwarf galaxies|ultra-faint dwarf galaxy]] and a [[List of Triangulum's suspected satellite galaxies|possible satellite]] of the [[Triangulum Galaxy]] ([[Messier object|Messier]] 33 or [[New General Catalogue|NGC]] 598). It is likely a small [[Elliptical galaxy|elliptical]] or [[Irregular galaxy|irregular]] galaxy, and is one of the [[Magnitude (astronomy)|faintest objects]] within the [[Local Group]], with an [[apparent magnitude]] of only 13.6. It was first reported in 2020 by [[Amateur astronomy|amateur astronomer]] [[Giuseppe Donatiello]] during a review of an [[astronomical survey]], and was later confirmed by the Italian [[Galileo National Telescope]], therefore making it first galaxy within the Local Group to have been discovered by an amateur astronomer.<ref name="secondDwarf">{{Cite journal |last1=Collins |first1=Michelle L. M. |last2=Martinez-Delgado |first2=David |last3=Monelli |first3=Matteo |last4=Tollerud |first4=Erik J. |last5=Donatiello |first5=Giuseppe |last6=Navabi |first6=Mahdieh |last7=Charles |first7=Emily |last8=Boschin |first8=Walter |date=18 January 2024 |title=Pisces VII/Triangulum III – M33's second dwarf satellite galaxy |url=https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/528/2/2614/7577614?login=false |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |volume=528 |issue=2 |pages=2614–2620 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stae199 |doi-access=free |via=Oxford Academic}}</ref>

== Discovery ==
Giuseppe Donatiello discovered Pisces VII in 2020 while analyzing the Dark Energy Camera Legacy Survey (DECaLS), a public [[Astronomical survey|astronomy survey]] conducted by the [[Cerro Tololo Inter-American Observatory]] using the [[Víctor M. Blanco Telescope|Blanco 4m]] telescope.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Luciani 0 |first=Massimo |date=2021-11-21 |title=Pisces VII is an ultra-faint dwarf galaxy whose discovery could be important in cosmological studies |url=https://english.tachyonbeam.com/2021/11/21/pisces-vii-is-an-ultra-faint-dwarf-galaxy-whose-discovery-could-be-important-in-cosmological-studies/ |access-date=2026-04-21 |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Survey |first=Legacy |date=2012-11-08 |title=The Dark Energy Camera Legacy Survey (DECaLS) |url=https://www.legacysurvey.org/decamls/ |access-date=2026-04-21 |website=Legacy Survey |language=en}}</ref> He reported it after identifying an unresolved over-density of stellar mass within the [[Pisces (constellation)|Pisces constellation]];<ref name="secondDwarf" /><ref name="discovery">{{Cite journal |last1=Martínez-Delgado |first1=David |last2=Karim |first2=Noushin |last3=Charles |first3=Emily J.E. |last4=Boschin |first4=Walter |last5=Monelli |first5=Matteo |last6=Collins |first6=Michelle L.M. |last7=Donatiello |first7=Giuseppe |last8=Alfaro |first8=Emilio J. |date=5 October 2021 |title=Pisces VII: discovery of a possible satellite of Messier 33 in the DESI legacy imaging surveys |url=https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/509/1/16/6381713?login=false |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |volume=509 |issue=1 |pages=16–24 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stab2797 |doi-access=free |via=Oxford Academic}}</ref> it was later confirmed by the Galileo National Telescope, making it the first Local Group object to have been discovered by an amateur astronomer.<ref name=uSurray>{{Cite news |date=17 November 2021 |title=Astronomical object found by amateur identified as new dwarf galaxy |url=https://www.surrey.ac.uk/news/astronomical-object-found-amateur-identified-new-dwarf-galaxy |access-date=21 April 2026 |work=University Of Surray}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Lazaro |first=Enrico de |date=2021-11-18 |title=Amateur Astronomer Discovers New Dwarf Galaxy {{!}} Sci.News |url=https://www.sci.news/astronomy/pisces-vii-dwarf-galaxy-10283.html |access-date=2026-04-21 |website=Sci.News: Breaking Science News |language=en-US}}</ref>

== Nomenclature ==
Pisces VII or Triangulum III received both names from its location in the [[night sky]]; Pisces VII is derived from the galaxy's [[namesake]] constellation, Pisces, and is abbreviated as "Psc VII";<ref name="secondDwarf" /> and Triangulum III, from its status as a suspected, unconfirmed satellite galaxy of Triangulum.<ref name="discovery" />

== Characteristics ==
Pisces VII is a small elliptical or irregular galaxy which is primarily composed of smaller, old [[Red dwarf|red-dwarf]] [[star]]s, with a small population of younger [[blue giant]]s; this has contributed to Pisces VII's unusually high apparent magnitude of {{Val|13.6|2}}, in spite of its relatively close proximity to [[Earth]], and makes it one of the faintest objects within the Local Group at a [[solar luminosity]] of only {{Val|2.2|e=4}} {{Solar luminosity|link=[[Solar luminosity]]}}, and an [[absolute magnitude]] of {{Val|-6.1|2}}.<ref name="secondDwarf" /><ref name="discovery" /> It orbits 97 ± 6 [[Parsec|kiloparsecs]] (316,372 ± 341.56 [[Light-year|light years]]) away from Triangulum's galactic center, lying primarily within the galaxy's [[Dark matter halo|dark-matter halo]].<ref name="secondDwarf" />

Due to the [[Galaxy#Types and morphology|slightly warped]] structure of Triangulum, it is theorized that it made a close [[Flyby (spaceflight)|flyby]] with the [[Andromeda Galaxy]] within the past few billion years,<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Tepper-García |first1=Thor |last2=Bland-Hawthorn |first2=Joss |last3=Li |first3=Di |date=2020-04-21 |title=The M31/M33 tidal interaction: a hydrodynamic simulation of the extended gas distribution |url=https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/493/4/5636/5721546 |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |language=en |volume=493 |issue=4 |pages=5636–5647 |doi=10.1093/mnras/staa317 |doi-access=free |issn=0035-8711|arxiv=1911.11911 }}</ref> and thus it is likely that much of the [[Interstellar medium|matter]] of Pisces VII was [[Tidal stripping|tidally stripped]] and pulled into Andromeda, leaving a much dimmer, fainter structure.<ref name=":0" />

== Observation ==
{{Location mark|image=Pisces constellation map.svg|alt=Map of the constellation Pisces|type=thumb|caption=Location of Pisces VII/Triangulum III in the constellation [[Pisces (constellation)|Pisces]] (circled)|mark=Red circle.svg|mark_width=6|x=283|y=271}}
Pisces VII is visible in the namesake constellation of Pisces at a right ascension of {{Right ascension|20|25|8.4}} and a declination of {{Declination|26|23|27.6}}, in the J2000 [[epoch]].<ref name=":0" /> Due to its high magnitude, it cannot be seen with the [[Limiting magnitude#Naked-eye visibility|naked human eye]], and is extremely difficult to resolve and observe with amateur telescopes.<ref name="discovery" />

